                "content": [
                    [
                    "All these various branches and parts<br>The Sage taught for wisdom's purpose<sup>*</sup> <i class='footnote'>1. This section introduces the chapter on wisdom/prajna, which explains how to increase the precious ultimate bodhicitta.<br>2. All the previously explained practices (generosity etc.) are like branches or limbs supporting wisdom.<br>3. The Buddha taught all these aspects as auxiliaries or supports for wisdom.<br>4. Just as armies support a king in battle, these practices support wisdom.<br>5. This is illustrated through scriptural citations comparing the perfections to rivers flowing into the ocean.<br>6. Without wisdom, the other perfections are like blind people without a guide.</i>",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "Regarding that, the ordinary worldly ones<br>Are refuted by the yogic worldly ones<sup>*</sup> <i class='footnote'>1. There are two types of ordinary worldly people: those untrained in philosophical systems and those trained in them<br>2. The untrained naturally cling to ideas of self and permanent identity<br>3. The trained non-Buddhist philosophers fall into either eternalism or nihilism<br>4. Buddhist yogis refute both these positions using reasoning<br>5. They show how the body is composed of parts (not unitary) and consciousness is impermanent<br>6. The commentary then details various Buddhist philosophical schools' views</i>",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "If nirvana is ultimate truth<br>And samsara conventional reality<br>Then even Buddhas would return to cycling<br>So what use is practicing enlightenment?<sup>*</sup> <i class='footnote'>1. An opponent (likely a Substantialist) raises an objection to the Madhyamaka view<br>2. They argue that if all phenomena are ultimately nirvana<br>3. And samsara exists only conventionally<br>4. Then nirvana and samsara would be identical<br>5. This would mean Buddhas would return to samsara<br>6. Therefore, they argue, there would be no purpose in practicing the bodhisattva path</i>",
                    "",
