                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "Like a dancer in different modes appearing,<br>If seen thus, it cannot be permanent.<br>If in different modes it stays the same thing,<br>Such oneness would be unprecedented.<sup>*</sup> <i class='footnote'>1. An opponent argues that consciousness maintains unity despite different perceptions<br>2. They use the analogy of a dancer appearing as different characters (deity in morning, yaksha in afternoon)<br>3. The text refutes this by pointing out that if something changes modes, it cannot be permanent<br>4. If something claims to maintain identity while changing modes, this would be an impossible type of unity<br>5. The commentary concludes that just as a dancer in different roles is actually different moments, consciousness in different states cannot be ultimately singular<br>6. If such unity were possible, everything would absurdly collapse into one</i>",
